            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/continuous+functions"} continuous functions
            between
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fixed+loci"} fixed loci
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+subgroups"} closed subgroups
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊂
                      %mi clsd
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H \subset_{clsd} G
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+model+structure+on+topological+spaces"} classical model structure on topological spaces
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalences"} weak homotopy equivalences
            or
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Serre+fibrations"} Serre fibrations
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+weak+homotopy+equivalences"} equivariant weak homotopy equivalences
          %p
            In the case that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+Lie+group"} compact Lie group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+theory"} homotopy theory
            coincides with that of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-CW+complexes"} G-CW complexes
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/localization"} localized
            at
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+function"} equivariant
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+equivalences"} homotopy equivalences
          %p
            For general
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Elmendorf%27s+theorem"} Elmendorf's theorem
            asserts that the fine equivariant model structure is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+equivalence"} Quillen equivalent
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/model+category+of+simplicial+presheaves"} model category of simplicial presheaves
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbit+category"} orbit category
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
          %p
            All this makes the fine model structure serve as a foundation for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+homotopy+theory"} equivariant homotopy theory
            and for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+cohomology"} equivariant cohomology
            in its refined form subsuming
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bredon+cohomology"} Bredon cohomology
          %p
            This is in contrast to the “coarse” or
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+model+structure"} Borel model structure
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+equivalences"} weak equivalences
            are simply the underlying
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+homotopy+equivalences"} weak homotopy equivalences
            (which need not restrict to weak homotopy equivalences on all
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fixed+loci"} fixed loci
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/slice+%28infinity%2C1%29-category"} slice
            homotopy theory over the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classifying+space"} classifying space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology"} cohomology
            of this coarse
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+homotopy+theory"} equivariant homotopy theory
            is just “Borel equivariant”, hence computes cohomology of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+constructions"} Borel constructions
          %p
            While one may, therefore, think of the fine model structure as exhibiting “genuine” equivariance (e.g.
            = succeed ")," do
              %a{:href => "#GuillouMayRubin13"} Guillou, May &amp; Rubin 13, p. 14-15
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+stable+homotopy+theory"} equivariant stable homotopy theory
            whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/G-spectra"} G-spectra
            are in addition equipped with “transfer maps”.
          %p
            However, when the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+subgroups"} closed subgroups
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            that enter the definition of the fine model structure are taken to be
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+groups"} compact groups
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+equivariant+homotopy+theory"} proper equivariant homotopy theory
            (conflating two usages of the term “proper”, but in a sensible way).
